ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".
# 10 OS Kernel Modules and Their Implementation

1. Priority-Based CPU Scheduling:

Add a priority value to each process and modify the scheduler to select runnable processes according to their priority.

2. Interprocess Communication (IPC):

Implement a mechanism such as message passing or pipes so processes can exchange data.

3. Memory Management Monitoring:

Track memory allocation and deallocation, and provide statistics about memory usage through a system call.

4. Process Monitoring and Statistics:

Track process IDs, states, and other process information, then display it using a user-level program.

5. Process Synchronization:

Use synchronization mechanisms, such as locks, to protect shared resources and prevent race conditions.

6. Timer and Sleep Management:

Use timer interrupts and process sleep/wakeup mechanisms to manage delays and wake sleeping processes at the appropriate time.

7. System Call Monitoring:

Modify the system-call handling mechanism to record the number or types of system calls executed.

8. CPU Utilization Monitoring:

Collect CPU activity statistics and calculate estimated CPU utilization for monitoring system performance.

9. File System Monitoring:

Track file operations such as opening, reading, writing, and closing files, and maintain operation statistics.

10. Kernel Error and Event Logging:

Record selected kernel events and errors in a controlled log to help identify problems and understand kernel behavior.